1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,220
Here's a shopping cart class.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:05,100
Objective-C, years old, and it works.

3
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:07,060
You've been asked to port it to Swift.

4
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:10,850
So you start with a test, and you stall on the first line.

5
00:00:11,170 --> 00:00:12,410
What should checkout return?

6
00:00:12,830 --> 00:00:13,650
Nobody knows.

7
00:00:13,990 --> 00:00:16,050
The people who decided have moved on.

8
00:00:16,510 --> 00:00:18,950
All anyone knows is what it does today.

9
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:21,080
That's the oracle problem.

10
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:25,380
A test needs something that knows right from wrong, and for legacy code,

11
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:26,760
nobody can write it down.

12
00:00:28,767 --> 00:00:31,007
Haruspec's answer is to stop asking.

13
00:00:31,367 --> 00:00:34,387
Whatever the old code does today is what correct means.

14
00:00:34,747 --> 00:00:37,087
So the old code becomes the oracle.

15
00:00:37,837 --> 00:00:42,437
Haruspec drives the old implementation and the new one with the same generated inputs.

16
00:00:42,717 --> 00:00:47,157
It records what each does at its boundary: every call and return,

17
00:00:47,517 --> 00:00:50,837
every notification it posts, and the state it leaves behind.

18
00:00:51,237 --> 00:00:52,637
Then it compares the two.

19
00:00:53,335 --> 00:00:54,115
No mocks.

20
00:00:54,395 --> 00:00:55,575
No assertions.

21
00:00:55,815 --> 00:00:57,155
No expected values.

22
00:00:57,515 --> 00:01:00,215
The legacy code is the expected value.

23
00:01:00,475 --> 00:01:02,035
That's differential testing.

24
00:01:02,785 --> 00:01:08,685
One honest caveat, stated up front: this is a best-effort oracle, not an omniscient one.

25
00:01:09,005 --> 00:01:11,165
Where it can't see, it says so.

26
00:01:13,167 --> 00:01:15,267
You'll reach for it in one of two shapes.

27
00:01:16,017 --> 00:01:17,377
The first is a campaign.

28
00:01:17,757 --> 00:01:22,977
You're rewriting something that mustn't change behavior: an Objective-C class moving to Swift,

29
00:01:23,177 --> 00:01:25,097
or a serial queue becoming an actor.

30
00:01:25,637 --> 00:01:28,037
The old code and its port live in the same build.

31
00:01:28,337 --> 00:01:32,357
The old one runs live as the baseline, and every port is checked against it,

32
00:01:32,577 --> 00:01:35,397
until the port ships and the old code is deleted.

33
00:01:36,147 --> 00:01:37,687
The second is continuous.

34
00:01:38,007 --> 00:01:42,307
There's one implementation and two builds: main, and your branch.

35
00:01:42,827 --> 00:01:47,507
Main's behavior is recorded once, as a plan, and your branch replays it,

36
00:01:47,507 --> 00:01:49,547
to catch changes nobody meant to make.

37
00:01:49,947 --> 00:01:53,647
When your branch merges, its behavior becomes the next baseline.

38
00:01:54,397 --> 00:01:56,317
The same spec drives both.

39
00:01:58,333 --> 00:02:01,133
Here's the whole authored surface for a small promo cart.

40
00:02:01,663 --> 00:02:02,603
Three operations.

41
00:02:03,083 --> 00:02:04,163
Add some coffee.

42
00:02:04,443 --> 00:02:05,483
Apply the promo.

43
00:02:05,863 --> 00:02:07,783
Check out, which always runs last.

44
00:02:08,063 --> 00:02:13,363
The coffee quantity is a seam: a value the oracle controls, swept from one to twenty.

45
00:02:13,363 --> 00:02:17,543
And the operations run in every order the spec allows, some of them twice.

46
00:02:18,293 --> 00:02:19,573
Then one line.

47
00:02:19,973 --> 00:02:21,893
The legacy cart is the baseline.

48
00:02:22,393 --> 00:02:24,233
Verify the Swift port against it.

49
00:02:24,983 --> 00:02:26,083
Notice what's missing.

50
00:02:26,423 --> 00:02:28,083
No expected total anywhere.

51
00:02:28,563 --> 00:02:31,623
Nobody worked out what ten coffees and a promo should cost.

52
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:37,753
Now suppose the port truncates the discount where the legacy code rounded it.

53
00:02:38,473 --> 00:02:43,053
At ten coffees, the two carts agree to the cent, so a test written at ten

54
00:02:43,053 --> 00:02:43,693
would pass.

55
00:02:44,433 --> 00:02:46,113
Haruspec doesn't stop at ten.

56
00:02:46,113 --> 00:02:49,773
Across a few hundred runs, the verdict comes back diverged.

57
00:02:50,491 --> 00:02:52,271
The port charges one cent more.

58
00:02:52,491 --> 00:02:56,791
The report names both places that shows: the total in the checkout notification,

59
00:02:57,071 --> 00:02:58,951
and the value checkout returns.

60
00:02:59,371 --> 00:03:02,331
Then the input that triggers it: a quantity of one.

61
00:03:02,941 --> 00:03:07,501
And beneath it, what the verdict was measured over: how much was explored,

62
00:03:07,821 --> 00:03:10,501
what was covered, and what the oracle could not see.

63
00:03:11,081 --> 00:03:14,481
Every verdict carries that block, green or red.

64
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:18,100
That's one of four verdicts.

65
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:20,820
Equivalent: every probe matched.

66
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,900
Diverged: behavior differs, and the report says where.

67
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:31,240
Cannot verify: capture or coverage fell short, so the oracle refuses to claim what it didn't

68
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:32,820
see, and names the gap.

69
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:38,740
Unstable: identical runs disagreed, usually because the subject made up a value itself,

70
00:03:39,060 --> 00:03:41,260
like a fresh identifier or a clock reading.

71
00:03:42,006 --> 00:03:42,986
Why four?

72
00:03:42,986 --> 00:03:47,026
Because the worst thing a tool like this can do is print green over behavior it

73
00:03:47,026 --> 00:03:47,546
never saw.

74
00:03:47,806 --> 00:03:49,586
A false alarm costs you an hour.

75
00:03:49,786 --> 00:03:52,546
A false green ships a bug past a check you trusted.

76
00:03:52,886 --> 00:03:55,246
That's why it would rather say cannot verify.

77
00:03:57,267 --> 00:03:59,207
Starting takes three steps.

78
00:04:00,027 --> 00:04:04,187
Add the package with Swift Package Manager, and depend on one product,

79
00:04:04,547 --> 00:04:05,247
Haruspec.

80
00:04:05,817 --> 00:04:06,677
Write a spec.

81
00:04:07,097 --> 00:04:12,937
Stamp the protocol your class already speaks, and its operations derive from its signatures.

82
00:04:13,337 --> 00:04:18,237
The spec says only what a signature can't: here, which promo codes matter,

83
00:04:18,477 --> 00:04:20,217
and that checkout runs last.

84
00:04:20,967 --> 00:04:21,647
Then check.

85
00:04:21,927 --> 00:04:25,887
In a test, bind the legacy class as the baseline and check the port.

86
00:04:26,087 --> 00:04:28,507
The port carries no Haruspec attribute.

87
00:04:29,167 --> 00:04:31,747
Conforming to the protocol is the whole adoption.

88
00:04:32,481 --> 00:04:37,681
For the continuous shape, opt the repository in once, mark the spec continuous,

89
00:04:38,161 --> 00:04:41,001
and run haruspec check on any branch.

90
00:04:41,541 --> 00:04:47,061
It records behavior where your branch left main, then replays only the specs your change touches,

91
00:04:47,061 --> 00:04:48,521
on your machine.

92
00:04:50,533 --> 00:04:52,753
That's Haruspec in five minutes.

93
00:04:52,753 --> 00:04:54,613
Three videos go deeper.

94
00:04:55,363 --> 00:04:57,823
Specs: the one thing you write.

95
00:04:58,383 --> 00:05:03,123
Seams: what the oracle controls, and why a value it doesn't control reads unstable.

96
00:05:03,923 --> 00:05:09,063
And verdicts: the four answers, the block that qualifies them, and how to declare the change

97
00:05:09,063 --> 00:05:09,863
you meant to make.

98
00:05:10,525 --> 00:05:12,565
And that test we couldn't finish?

99
00:05:12,845 --> 00:05:15,245
We never had to know what checkout should return.

100
00:05:15,565 --> 00:05:17,305
The old code already knew.
